herdr-fleet

This project is derived from herdr-web-ui by devswha, licensed under the MIT License
(see LICENSE for the original copyright and permission notice).

Upstream:    https://github.com/devswha/herdr-web-ui
Base commit: 6bcb9bca4980b5a21b563dafac805a6a6c39ed61 (v0.3.45)

The upstream history was not imported. Upstream changes are not merged
automatically; they are reviewed and ported by hand.

agents/architect.md adapts ideas from pstack's architect skill (grounding first,
designing twice, the design red flags), by Lauren Tan, licensed under the MIT License.

Source:      https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/pstack/skills/architect

The prompt rail (src/lib/promptRail.ts, src/components/PromptRail.tsx) takes ideas from
oikon48's prompt-rail Claude Code plugin, Copyright (c) 2026 oikon48, licensed under the
MIT License: a rail of the person's prompts with a thick tick for the one being read, the
turn line's form ("1m 23s · 2 edits · app.ts", up to three files written, two files with
one name told apart by their folder), and the search rule (the newest prompt holding every
word). The code was written for this project, except fileNames in src/lib/promptRail.ts,
which follows the plugin's function of that name closely.

Source:      https://github.com/oikon48/prompt-rail/tree/main/plugins/prompt-rail

The thread plan (shared/plan.ts, the Plan tab, agents/lead.md) takes its shape from the
html-plan plugin's idea: a plan as a tree of claims (what, how, where), one exhibit per claim,
each decision on the claim it changes, and one response sent back. No code of it is used; the
plan is JSON the Web UI draws.

Source:      https://github.com/anthropics/claude-plugins-community/tree/main/html-plan

src/lib/plainQuestion.ts (the plain-text question test) and server/explain.ts (the
explanation's four sections, the quoted-data instruction and the bounds on what is
sent) adapt qa-guide from claude_qamods, Copyright (c) 2026 aieo-product, licensed
under the MIT License.

Source:      https://github.com/aieo-product/claude_qamods/tree/main/plugins/qa-guide

rust/crates/screen/src/text/width.rs and rust/crates/screen/src/text/width/table.rs copy
Bun.stringWidth of Bun 1.4.4 (tag bun-v1.4.4, commit 38afee6c84fcf48c0edb2466aa4b27d287a59c30),
by the Bun authors (oven-sh), licensed under the MIT License: the grapheme break rules, the
grapheme widths of GraphemeState, the characters Bun counts in bulk runs, and the table of
src/jsc/bindings/stringWidthTables.h, rewritten as runs of code points. Bun's LICENSE.md states
"Bun itself is MIT-licensed." and names no copyright holder. The escape sequence reader in
rust/crates/screen/src/text.rs follows consumeANSI (src/jsc/bindings/ANSIHelpers.h) of the same
version.

Source:      https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/tree/bun-v1.4.4

Bun ported those grapheme break rules from uucode, Copyright (c) 2026 Jacob Sandlund, licensed
under the MIT License.

Source:      https://github.com/jacobsandlund/uucode

Bun generated that table from the Unicode Character Database 17.0.0 (EastAsianWidth.txt,
extracted/DerivedGeneralCategory.txt, emoji/emoji-data.txt), © 2025 Unicode, Inc., licensed
under the Unicode License v3 (https://www.unicode.org/license.txt).

Source:      https://www.unicode.org/Public/17.0.0/ucd/

